          "title": "You'll want to know: أمير",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "أمير — amīr — \"a prince; an emir\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "كان في المدينة أمير شاب. kāna fī al-madīna amīr shābb. There was a young prince in the city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural: أمراء, umarāʾ."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The feminine is أميرة, a princess."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "أمير (amīr) is built on the native Arabic root ʾ-m-r (Lane), \"to command\", the root of amr, an order. An amīr is one who commands; English emir and admiral are usually traced to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
